    difficulty: "medium",
    date: "2024-11-01",
    text: "But on Oct 31, the matinee show at 3pm was half empty. The news was not yet out officially but everyone seemed to know of the attempt on PM Indira Gandhi life. An air of uncertainty prevailed. Everyone wondered whether she would survive. Even during the interval, one could hear gatekeepers discuss the news in hushed tones. By the time I came out of the theatre, All India Radio had announced the news of Indira assassination by her Sikh bodyguards. The road was choked. Vehicles honked madly, an uncommon occurrence those days. Everyone looked dazed and confused. The winter was slowly setting in and it was completely dark around 6.30pm. I walked to the nearest bus stand. About 60-70 passengers were waiting for DTC buses, the primary mode of public transport those days. There were none. After a 45-minute wait, 521, which took you to Super Bazar in Connaught Place, came. It was packed but some of us managed to squeeze in. A young woman in salwar-kameez was crying like a child. She kept on repeating, Ab desh kaise chalega? Who will run the country now? Others kept nodding in affirmation and baying for the blood of Khalistanis. The three or four Sikh passengers looked extremely worried. When the bus reached near Kotla, we had a first glimpse of what lay in store. The air was thick with black smoke. From Kotla to Janpath, dozens of cars and scooters were either in flames or lying abandoned. The brave driver kept changing the route to reach the destination. After every few minutes one could see belligerent crowds with sticks and bats on the road stopping vehicles. Little before the swank Le Meridien Hotel on Janpath, arsonists stopped our bus. They hurled abuses against Bhindrawale and Khalistanis. They did not cover their faces. They moved menacingly from one end of the bus to another in search of prey. Thankfully, the Sikh passengers had got off earlier. A car passing by told our driver to avoid going to CP, barely half a km away. No bus and no auto-rickshaws either I was stranded and scared. I walked to CP. Designed by British architect Robert Tor Russell, CP was named after Prince Arthur, the first Duke of Connaught and a grandson of Queen Victoria. Since its completion in 1933, it had been the capital finest shopping and business district and a premier attraction for tourists. That night it looked so different. Many shops were on fire. The streets were littered with stones and shattered glass. Vehicles burnt like paper. Stench of smoke filled the air. Law and order had surrendered to the merchants of death, who were calling the shots. Not a single cop was seen.\n\nIn the operational architecture of Uttar Pradesh Police, technological adaptation has transformed administrative transparency and tactical responsiveness. The integration of modern analytical dashboards enables precinct supervisors to track incident lifecycles from emergency phone reporting through forensic evidence collection and final court submission. Computer operators stationed at district control centers must process voluminous textual transmissions including daily case diaries, special surveillance bulletins, and statutory notices under stringent time constraints.\n\nTypographical precision remains foundational to digital governance within law enforcement agencies. Any transcription oversight or inadvertent omission during records entry can compromise the legal validity of prosecutorial documentation or cause administrative delays during judicial hearings. Candidates preparing for state recruitment examinations are therefore evaluated not merely on gross finger velocity across the keyboard, but fundamentally on sustained concentration, typographical discipline, and the ability to maintain above eighty-five percent accuracy throughout the full fifteen-minute testing interval. Consistent keyboard drills and systematic text auditing cultivate the motor memory necessary for high-speed administrative documentation."
