    id: "ar-7482",
    testNumber: 63,
    title: "Test #63: SSC Typing Passage - 01/12/2019 - Batch III",
    language: "english",
    category: "up_police_co",
    categoryGroup: "up_govt",
    duration: 15,
    wordCount: 629,
    difficulty: "medium",
    date: "2024-10-23",
    text: "The value of education lies not just in getting a degree or training for a job. The real value is in giving back to society, and in helping those less privileged and less fortunate than us. It is with this sentiment that Guru Nanak College has extended a special facility in partnership with an NGO that runs a school for special children, that is children with special needs. I am told that certificate courses are offered to such students to help them find employment and to promote mainstreaming. I wish such efforts all success. Your College also strives to inculcate service to society and make students socially responsible. Following the December 2015 floods in Chennai, I have learnt that a team from Guru Nanak College was one of the first to reach people in distress and provide food, water, medicines and other essentials. The University of Madras has both benefited from and contributed to a tradition of scholarship that is central to Tamil Nadu identity. Even ordinary families in the state emphasise the value of education. This has had a direct impact on social development indices and on the economy. Tamil Nadu has a proud culture of research and innovation, whether in the pure sciences or in medicine and of engineering and manufacturing. It has a well-regarded IT sector and a growing digital economy. These are complemented by excellence in the humanities. It is no surprise that the Tamil language is among the most ancient in the world. Centuries before many others, it was exploring the literature and philosophy of learning. The ability to pursue education as an end in itself as well as to help fill gaps in day-to- day lives of our fellow citizens is commendable. In this context the people of Tamil Nadu, and institutions such as the University of Madras, are a model for our country. Inevitably, it is to institutions such as these that we turn for direction and leadership at the beginning of the 21st century. This is an exciting chapter for India. We are looking to become a developed society and to urgently eliminating poverty as well as ensuring healthcare, education, housing and energy access for all our people. Simultaneously, our country is seeking to meet the opportunities as well as the challenges of the Fourth Industrial Revolution of robotics, genomics and Artificial Intelligence. Universities such as this one are expected to be our navigators on this journey. Institutions in Tamil Nadu have shown commendable agility in nurturing an egosystem that helps engineers and other qualified young people to become entrepreneurs and job creators. This is the route to the future and this is the pathway for the youth of our country. This convocation and this anniversary are a moment of renewal for both the University of Madras and for those graduating today.\n\nIn the operational architecture of Uttar Pradesh Police, technological adaptation has transformed administrative transparency and tactical responsiveness. The integration of modern analytical dashboards enables precinct supervisors to track incident lifecycles from emergency phone reporting through forensic evidence collection and final court submission. Computer operators stationed at district control centers must process voluminous textual transmissions including daily case diaries, special surveillance bulletins, and statutory notices under stringent time constraints.\n\nTypographical precision remains foundational to digital governance within law enforcement agencies. Any transcription oversight or inadvertent omission during records entry can compromise the legal validity of prosecutorial documentation or cause administrative delays during judicial hearings. Candidates preparing for state recruitment examinations are therefore evaluated not merely on gross finger velocity across the keyboard, but fundamentally on sustained concentration, typographical discipline, and the ability to maintain above eighty-five percent accuracy throughout the full fifteen-minute testing interval. Consistent keyboard drills and systematic text auditing cultivate the motor memory necessary for high-speed administrative documentation."
