    duration: 15,
    wordCount: 564,
    difficulty: "medium",
    date: "2024-09-24",
    text: "The Finance Commission grants to urban local bodies are very crucial for the development of public amenities in the municipalities. Under 14th Finance Commission, the total grant given to the urban local bodies was Rs.3,000 crore, and out of this, Rs. 581 crore are still pending. The Government of Andhra Pradesh has submitted the required Utilization Certificates for the released grants and also demonstrated compliances for all reforms stipulated by the Central Finance Commission, that is up to date annual accounts, and improvement in municipal revenues. These amounts are very crucial to develop infrastructure, roads, water supply, protection of open spaces, and development of playgrounds. The State of Andhra Pradesh is facing severe financial stress due to bifurcation and COVID-19 pandemic. Hence, I would request the Central Government, through you, Sir, to release the pending Rs. 581 crore at the earliest. Sir, my parliamentary constituency is called the second Banaras. It is situated at the confluence of the rivers Tel, Ong and Mahanadi. The districts under my constituency have several ancient temples of historical, architectural, and religious importance dating from the 8th to the 13th century including Samadhi Pitha of the famous poet saint of Odisha whose poem is also inscribed in our Parliament as well as the United Nations Organization, ancient pre-historic caves where the Goddess has been worshipped by the tribal people since time immemorial. There are many temples in these districts. These temples need to be preserved and developed into places of heritage and religious tourism which will help the economy of the districts and generate employment opportunities. As we all know, Balangir is an aspirational district. Both these districts are home to the famous Sambalpuri weavers. I would request the hon. Minister of Tourism to include these two districts of Odisha, under the Swadesh Darshan Scheme, as heritage, spiritual or rural circuit. I would like to draw the attention of this House to the recent controversies regarding censure of free speech by social media platforms like Facebook, Twitter, and their affiliates in India. This poses a significant constitutional challenge not only on the grounds of unreasonable restriction of free speech but also amounts to illegal interference during elections. Sir, Facebook, Twitter and similar foreign social media platforms claim themselves to be intermediaries under the IT Act. However, the key element of this definition is that the role of the intermediaries is limited to processing, storing and transmitting data of third-party users.\n\nIn the operational architecture of Uttar Pradesh Police, technological adaptation has transformed administrative transparency and tactical responsiveness. The integration of modern analytical dashboards enables precinct supervisors to track incident lifecycles from emergency phone reporting through forensic evidence collection and final court submission. Computer operators stationed at district control centers must process voluminous textual transmissions including daily case diaries, special surveillance bulletins, and statutory notices under stringent time constraints.\n\nTypographical precision remains foundational to digital governance within law enforcement agencies. Any transcription oversight or inadvertent omission during records entry can compromise the legal validity of prosecutorial documentation or cause administrative delays during judicial hearings. Candidates preparing for state recruitment examinations are therefore evaluated not merely on gross finger velocity across the keyboard, but fundamentally on sustained concentration, typographical discipline, and the ability to maintain above eighty-five percent accuracy throughout the full fifteen-minute testing interval. Consistent keyboard drills and systematic text auditing cultivate the motor memory necessary for high-speed administrative documentation."
