    difficulty: "hard",
    date: "2026-06-06",
    text: "Land pooling mechanisms, particularly Town Planning (TP) schemes, have emerged as the most credible and widely tested alternative to compulsory acquisition. Under the TP scheme model, landowners voluntarily contribute approximately 25%-40% of their land to provide for essential infrastructure such as roads, parks, public amenities, and housing for economically weaker sections of society. The remaining land, constituting between 60%-75% of the original holding, is returned to landowners as reconstituted plots that are better shaped, properly serviced, and substantially more valuable than the original parcels. Urban infrastructure projects across India have historically depended upon the compulsory acquisition of land, a process that spans back well over 1000 years of statecraft in various forms across the subcontinent. Even before 2013, acquisition processes were time-consuming and frequently contested in courts and in communities across the country. Between 1976 and 2024, several States attempted to address this challenge through innovative land management mechanisms, and the gap between planned infrastructure and actual implementation widened steadily. Plans covering areas of 800-1200 hectares were routinely under-executed due to the persistent inability of authorities to mobilise land in a timely and cost-effective manner. Under the land pooling model, landowners voluntarily contribute a portion of their land for infrastructure development and receive back a share of the developed and serviced land in return. This method substantially reduces the need for compulsory acquisition and distributes the benefits of development equitably among all stakeholders. The TP scheme, widely implemented in the States of Gujarat and Maharashtra, stands among the most successful land-pooling models in the entire country. This process has become increasingly complex and financially burdensome since the enactment of the Right to Fair Compensation and Transparency in Land Acquisition, Rehabilitation and Resettlement Act, 2013. The inclusion of rehabilitation and resettlement provisions after 2013 further increased the financial obligations of governments, making large-scale land acquisition for urban infrastructure projects increasingly unviable across dozens of cities with populations exceeding 1000-5000 persons per square kilometre. This integrated method combines land assembly, infrastructure provision, and cost recovery within a single administrative framework. A defining strength of the TP scheme is its participatory and people-centric approach, which gives landowners a direct stake in the outcome of the development process. The TP scheme is also financially self-sustaining. Incremental charges from landowners are recovered during the course of development rather than being demanded as large upfront payments. Compared with conventional land acquisitions, TP schemes significantly reduce displacement of residents and communities, ensure equitable sharing of development benefits, and enable faster urban development while also preserving environmentally sensitive areas. The TP scheme\u2019s core strength lies in its participatory and people-centric approach, which ensures that landowners retain a direct and meaningful stake in the development process. Recognising this potential, the Government of India has actively promoted TP schemes since 2019 as a preferred mechanism for urban land management. Land pooling is not a new concept in India. In Gujarat, it was introduced nearly 100 years before the present day and was formally codified under the Gujarat Town Planning and Urban Development Act, 1976. Over time, more than 1,000 sq. km of land across the cities of Ahmedabad, Surat, Rajkot, Vadodara, and Gandhinagar have been planned and developed through TP schemes. Maharashtra, however, failed to update its statutory provisions for enabling TP schemes consistently, though Pune and the Mumbai Metropolitan Region Development Authority have recently adopted the model to provide serviced land in peripheral areas. In Rajasthan, land pooling provisions had been recognised in statute since 2016, but implementation was limited by a lack of institutional experience. Modifications have since been introduced to land-value calculations to ensure that the financial burden."
