    wordCount: 643,
    difficulty: "medium",
    date: "2026-06-06",
    text: "The Strait of Hormuz, a narrow corridor just 33 km wide at its most constricted point, connects the Persian Gulf to the Gulf of Oman and ultimately to the Arabian Sea. It is the world\u2019s single most critical oil transit chokepoint, through which, during 2023-2025, approximately 20% of the world\u2019s liquefied natural gas and 25% of all seaborne oil passed every year. In 2025 alone, 20 million barrels of oil and oil products moved through this route every single day, according to the U.S. Energy Information Administration. Around 3,000 ships sailed through the strait each month before the outbreak of the war in early 2026, and there are 8 major islands within the strait, 7 of which are controlled by Iran. Iran\u2019s physical command over the strait is not merely political it is deeply geographical. The weaponisation of the strait is not without historical precedent. During the 1980s, across the eight years of the Iran-Iraq war, both sides systematically targeted fuel tankers moving through Persian Gulf waters, turning a commercial waterway into a theatre of asymmetric conflict. In more recent years, Iran repeatedly warned the international community that it would close the strait if it came under military attack. When the United States and Israel launched a war on February 28, 2026, beginning with the assassination of Iran\u2019s Supreme Leader Ayatollah Ali Khamenei, Tehran responded exactly as it had promised. It retaliated against Israel and Arab nations in the Gulf, and it took effective control of the Strait of Hormuz. The number of ships passing through the strait daily, which had exceeded 100 before the war, fell by over 90% almost immediately, sending shockwaves through global energy markets, insurance sectors, and the economies of energy-import-dependent nations. India, one of the world\u2019s largest importers of Gulf oil, was among the countries hit hardest by the resulting fuel price surge and supply uncertainty. The strait\u2019s strategic significance grew steadily after oil was discovered across the Persian Gulf region in the early 20th century. The post-War oil boom transformed the region fundamentally and irreversibly. All Persian Gulf nations Iran, Iraq, Saudi Arabia, Oman, Qatar, the UAE, Bahrain, and Kuwait have depended on the strait for access to the Arabian Sea and to global markets. While Iran, Saudi Arabia, the UAE, and Oman possess ports outside Gulf waters, several smaller nations are entirely dependent on Hormuz for both exports and essential imports including food, medicine, and technology products. Hormuz is also a key corridor for fertiliser exports from the Gulf region, making its disruption a matter not only of energy security but of food security for many import-dependent nations across Asia and Africa. During the 40 days of intensive bombing, the United States and Israel inflicted substantial material damage on Iran\u2019s aerial and naval infrastructure. Yet these strikes proved insufficient to compel Iran to reopen the strait. Iran deployed cheap, asymmetric, and disruptive weapons to impose disproportionate costs on vessels that attempted to pass without permission from the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps. Several ships that tried to navigate the strait without IRGC clearance came under direct attack. Fuel prices climbed sharply. Insurance and shipping costs soared to levels not seen in decades. When U.S. President Donald Trump announced a ceasefire with Iran on April 8, 2026, he declared that Iran had agreed to reopen the Strait of Hormuz. The truce came into effect, but both sides held fundamentally different interpretations of its terms. Iran demanded a simultaneous ceasefire in Lebanon, while Israel intensified its bombing campaign there instead. When Iran refused to reopen the strait under these conditions, Mr. Trump announced a blockade of Iranian ports on April 12, barring Iranian vessels from passing through American-controlled waters in the Gulf of Oman. Iran\u2019s response was to cancel direct negotiations with the United States and to formally establish a new regulatory body the Persian Gulf Strait Authority (PGSA)."
