    difficulty: "medium",
    date: "2026-06-06",
    text: "The Agrometeorologist, says that rising temperatures and erratic rainfall now expose fruit crops to multiple simultaneous stresses. Every summer, the orchards of Muzaffarpur in Bihar fill with a heavy, sweet scent the unmistakable promise of ripening litchi. The Shahi litchi, prized for its succulent white flesh and delicate flavour, is a GI-tagged variety that has defined the identity of this region for well over 1,000 years. In 2024-25, the litchi crop had already shown signs of stress, but the season of 2025-26 proved far more devastating. Scientists estimate that nearly 70% of Bihar\u2019s litchi crop has been wiped out this year, a staggering loss running into hundreds of crores of rupees. Vegetables and Flowers Growers Association, estimated that the state\u2019s apple economy could shrink from Rs 5,000-6,000 crore to just Rs 1,500-2,000 crore this season. Temperatures breached 38-DegreeC during April and May, causing massive, premature fruit drop. The same erratic weather dry westerly winds that blackened the skin, dried up the pulp, and cracked the fruit was then followed by unseasonal rain and hailstorms that created conditions perfect for an aggressive outbreak of the litchi stink bug. This almost dystopian scene, described by helpless farmers, has been caused by the combination of unseasonal rain in the last two months and the destructive attack of the stink bug pest, a problem that local scientists say has grown beyond anything ever recorded. Last year, farmer was able to use earnings from the harvest to pay for his daughter\u2019s school fees and wedding expenses for another daughter. This year, he has nothing. The damage is only compounded by the fact that horticulture farming is capital-intensive and a far riskier endeavour than typical cropping, leaving farmers with little cushion when things go wrong. Summer once meant certainty in the orchards of Maharashtra. Mango trees in Konkan would bend under the weight of the Alphonso fruit, banana growers in Jalgaon would ready truckloads for markets across north India, and vineyards in Nashik would race against time to meet export deadlines. This year, many farmers say the season has instilled fear and unease. Agriculture department studies showed widespread losses in north Maharashtra, especially in Nashik and Dhule districts, while mango growers in Konkan reported one of the weakest harvests in recent years. In the Konkan belt, Alphonso farmers say flowering patterns have become erratic due to warmer winters, while unseasonal rain and humidity trigger fungal attacks and fruit drop. Nearly half the bunches cracked or developed fungus after rain. Export agents rejected the crop, saying it was not fit for Europe. \"One unseasonal spell of rain is enough to destroy an entire year of work,\" he said. Himachal Pradesh looks headed for one of its worst apple seasons in memory, with farmers reporting losses of up to 70% due to prolonged erratic weather. Traditional varieties such as Royal Delicious and Red Delicious are among the worst hit, with damage reaching 80% in some areas. Apple orchards did not receive the requisite chilling hours due to delayed and comparatively less snowfall, affecting bud development and the fruitsetting cycle. Scientists and agriculture experts agree that adaptation not short-term compensation will determine whether fruit farming in India remains viable. ICAR-Central Institute for Subtropical Horticulture director T. Damodaran says that India\u2019s litchi production is expected to go down by 40% because of crop loss in Bihar alone. The Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR) has, in the last decade, released 2,177 crop varieties targeting various climate vulnerabilities. India is now developing climate-resilient varieties HRMN-99, Anna, and Golden Dorset for apples, and Arka Udaya and Arka Suprabhath as varieties of mango that can cope with unpredictable weather and boost farm productivity. Studies have shown how techniques like cluster spraying and lowering canopy size have been effective in combating the stink bug on litchi fruit."
