    testNumber: 69,
    title: "Test #69: Could Aliens Reach Earth? - Physics Say No",
    language: "english",
    category: "ssb_hcm",
    categoryGroup: "defense",
    examSlug: "ssb-hcm-english-typing-skill-test",
    duration: 10,
    wordCount: 661,
    difficulty: "medium",
    date: "2026-06-06",
    text: "On May 22, 2026, the Pentagon released a second batch of declassified photos and videos showing what appear to be unexplained flying objects. But to assess the plausibility of alien visitors, it\u2019s necessary to understand the obstacles that an extraterrestrial vessel would need to overcome to reach Earth. No object can reach or exceed light speed (roughly 3,00,000 km per second). But well before that threshold, engineering constraints begin to assert themselves. Limited fuel and potential for structural damage will restrict a craft\u2019s speed. There is no universally accepted upper limit on interstellar flight speeds, but studies tend to converge around 30,000 km/s 10% of the speed of light as a realistic cruise velocity. At this speed, a journey of 10 light years will take roughly 100 years. There is no evidence of alien life in our solar system. So, visitors would likely have to come from another star system in the Milky Way. Proxima Centauri, the star closest to our Sun, is about 4.25 lightyears away. Since only a fraction of stars are thought to host intelligent life, the nearest alien civilization if one exists is surely much farther away than Proxima. Interstellar space offers some advantages. Lack of atmosphere means there is no drag. So, when the ship reaches its cruise speed, it can shut its propulsion system and coast to the final destination. But lack of atmosphere also means there is nothing to slow the ship down. Ideally, the propulsion system would have to both accelerate and decelerate. One propulsion strategy employs a high-powered laser to push a ship through space. The beam is projected from a stationary array near the home planet and directed toward a thin reflective sail attached to the ship. The beam\u2019s photons exert radiation pressure on the sail, propelling the ship forward. This approach requires no onboard fuel. But the energy needed to operate the laser would be staggering. Beamed propulsion also provides no mechanism for deceleration. At best, this method could be deployed as part of a hybrid strategy that uses a separate system for deceleration. A more practical solution is rocket propulsion. Rockets generate thrust by expelling high-velocity exhaust. By reversing the direction of the exhaust, rockets can also be used to slow the ship down. But their main disadvantage is that rockets must carry their own fuel in addition to passengers, habitat and lifesustaining systems. In other words, you need fuel to transport your fuel. All human missions have used chemical rocket propulsion, but a craft travelling at 30,000 km/s would need more fuel than all the mass in the observable universe. Antimatter propulsion is theoretically the most efficient option. When antimatter comes into contact with ordinary matter, the two undergo mutual annihilation and 100% of their combined mass is converted into energy. This makes it possible to achieve the same cruise velocity one-tenth the speed of light with fuel accounting for less than a quarter of the ship\u2019s total mass. The downside is that antimatter is extremely unstable and difficult to make. To date, particle physicists have produced less than 20 billionths of a gram of antimatter. Moreover, these particles had lifespans lasting only fractions of a second and a price tag in the hundreds of millions of dollars. Nuclear fusion offers a more viable alternative. Fusion engines remain aspirational, but they could, in theory, produce 10 million times more energy per kilogram than chemical rockets. Still, a fusion-powered ship with a cruise velocity of 30,000 km/s would need fuel equivalent to 150 times the mass of the ship itself. We now come to ship design. Interstellar space has a sparse smattering of microscopic cosmic dust. At 30,000 km/s, the tiny grains would hit the hull with the energy of a .22-calibre bullet. Surviving the onslaught would require no less than a fortress with complex shielding, increasing the total mass of the ship and driving up fuel use. No single law of physics prohibits an interstellar voyage to Earth."
