    date: "2026-10-24",
    text: "Yogi Adityanath asked graduating law students at the 5th-convocation of Dr Ram Manohar Lohiya National Law University (RMLNLU) to treat their degrees as a \u2018Vakalatnama\u2019 for 140-crore Indians and make a case for a \u2018Viksit Bharat@2047\u2019, saying those who believe such a transformation is impossible are the real opponents of the vision. Drawing a parallel with courtroom proceedings, Adityanath said that if a case for a developed India in 2047 were to be filed, the country\u2019s 140 crore citizens would be the petitioners and those with a status quo mindset would be the respondents. In a constitutional democracy, legal professionals sustain balance between power and accountability and between liberty and collective good. \u201cWho is the respondent in this case? It is not any specific individual or political party, but rather those people who are \u2018Yathasthiti Vaadi\u2019, people who have already assumed that this transformation is impossible,\u201d he said, asking the graduates to consider how they would present their case before people unwilling to accept arguments or evidence. He cited the repeal of more than 1,500 obsolete laws, amendments to 183 provisions across 42 laws through the Jan Vishwas Act, and reforms such as the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code and the Goods and Services Tax. He also referred to the abrogation of Article-370 and the Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam, saying reforms involved not only enacting new laws but also removing those that had become irrelevant. Lawyers are not mouthpieces of a client\u2019s cause but officers of court and guardians of rule of law. Invoking 2014 as a turning point in the country\u2019s reform process, the Chief Minister said India had moved from being counted among the \u2018Fragile Five\u2019 economies to becoming one of the world\u2019s top five economies. Recalling the Emergency imposed in 1975, Adityanath said it had shown the country the difference between the \u201crule of law\u201d and the \u201cruler of law\u201d. Referring to Uttar Pradesh, he said the state had moved from the \u201crule of mafia\u201d to the \u201crule of law\u201d under his government. Before 2017, he alleged, mafia figures wielded such influence that even convoys of people holding administrative and constitutional positions would stop in front of a mafia figure in Prayagraj. He said change had helped transform the state\u2019s image and its economic standing. He asked the graduates to make public trust, protection of rights and access to justice the foundation of their professional lives. Every person, including those at the lowest rung of society, should be able to access justice without discrimination, he said. Quoting the Sanskrit maxim \u2018Satyam Vada, Dharmam Chara, Swadhyayanma Pramadaha\u2019 (speak the truth, practise righteousness and never be negligent in your studies), he described it as a guiding principle for legal professionals. On artificial intelligence, Adityanath acknowledged its usefulness in legal research, drafting and data analysis but cautioned against treating it as a substitute for human judgment. He also flagged risks posed by deepfakes, saying the authenticity of digital evidence needed scientific examination. \u201cMake AI an assistant, not a charioteer. Your judgment and Dharma should be your charioteer,\u201d he said, adding that while a machine could prepare a draft, a legal professional remained accountable for its consequences. Supreme Court judge Justice Vikram Nath, Allahabad high court Chief Justice Arun Bhansali, state higher education minister Yogendra Upadhyay and university vice-chancellor Professor Amarpal Singh were among those present. Supreme Court Justice Vikram Nath has said that academic degrees mark the beginning of a larger journey rather than a final destination. The chief justice of Allahabad high court, Arun Bhansali, while addressing the gathering, reminded students of the university motto \u2018Dharm Sansthapanaarth\u2019 for the establishment of dharma that legal education aims at strengthening a just social order. Laying a four-point roadmap, he said that integrity must remain non-negotiable amid commercialisation;"
