    wordCount: 573,
    difficulty: "hard",
    date: "2026-10-18",
    text: "More than a-century ago, when large sections of Indian Muslims were struggling to reconcile traditional learning with the demands of a rapidly changing world, Sir Syed Ahmad Khan saw education as the bridge between the two. A reformer, educationist and champion of scientific thought, he believed that a community unwilling to embrace modern knowledge risked being left behind. As his 209th birth anniversary is observed on Oct-17, his call for education, rational inquiry and social reform remains remarkably relevant. The most enduring institutional legacy of that vision is Aligarh Muslim University (AMU). In 1864, he founded the Scientific Society, with the objective of making modern knowledge more accessible through translations into Urdu. Decades later, the Constitution of India would include the development of scientific temper among the fundamental duties of citizens under Article-51A. Sir Syed firmly believed that a Muslim equipped with modern education and knowledge could become an asset to the country and contribute meaningfully to nation-building. Without education, he feared, the community would struggle to participate fully in economic and national progress. His emphasis on scientific thinking was strikingly forward-looking. Over the decades, the university has produced scientists, litterateurs, sportspersons and professionals who have distinguished themselves in their respective fields and contributed to the country. Sir Syed pioneered the Aligarh Movement, aimed at modernising education among Muslims in the 19th century. During his eventful career, he also served in the judicial and administrative system, including as a sub-judge. In the aftermath of the Industrial Revolution, the world was evolving rapidly in education, science and industry. The Muslim community, however, remained inadequately equipped to keep pace with these developments. This educational backwardness was among Sir Syed\u2019s biggest concerns. In the 19th century, apart from religious instruction, Muslim education relied heavily on classical works such as Bostan and Gulistan, two celebrated works of Persian literature by Sheikh Saadi Shirazi. These were, undoubtedly, great works of literature, wisdom and moral philosophy, but contemporary education also needed greater engagement with science and economics to prepare the community for the approaching 20th century. Sir Syed\u2019s vision was that Muslims should break the shackles of orthodoxy and provide their children with modern scientific education. A famous couplet aptly captures the dilemma confronting society: To be afraid of new ways and stubbornly cling to old customs is indeed a difficult stage in the life of nations. Sir Syed not only advocated modern education but also championed scientific rationalism and supported women\u2019s education. The foundation of Muhammadan Anglo-Oriental College in 1877 marked a significant milestone in the history of modern education in India. Its origins can be traced to an educational institution established at Aligarh in 1875. Inspired by the universities of Oxford and Cambridge, Sir Syed sought to create an environment where traditional values could coexist with scientific knowledge and intellectual freedom. His efforts extended beyond classrooms, encouraging debate, independent thinking and social responsibility. In 1870, he launched the journal Tehzibul Akhlaq to promote social awareness and encourage reform within the community. Recognising the importance of collective participation, he established the Muhammadan Educational Conference in 1886 to advance educational opportunities and promote cooperation. His vision gradually attracted scholars, teachers and reformers who shared his commitment to intellectual development. Following his death in 1898, the movement continued to inspire educational initiatives across the country. In 1920, Muhammadan Anglo-Oriental College became Aligarh Muslim University, fulfilling an important institutional objective of the movement. Today, his educational philosophy offers a valuable reminder."
